## Decisions

### D22: Direct physics facade eviction

Problem: SHINOBU still imported `Hecton8.Physics` and sent KCC velocity through `PhysicsDeterminismSignals`.
Solution: Publish finite-sanitized `KccVelocitySignal` directly to the typed unmanaged signal lane with a local sequence.
Rejected Alternatives: Keeping the physics facade preserves a side cache but keeps SHINOBU compiled against a sibling runtime.
Scalability potential: Low/Middle/High/Ultra consume one typed lane; visual overkill stays downstream.
Hardware Impact: Removes one post-fixed facade hop. Real microseconds are PENDING PROFILER.

### D23: Fixed-step cold I/O firewall

Problem: Lazy `EnsureNativeState()` could run archive scans from fixed-step callers.
Solution: Callers now pass `allowColdInitialization`; `FixedTick` and `GetStateRef` use `false`, while cold/slow/service paths use `true`.
Rejected Alternatives: Lazy fixed-step archive scanning risks MicroSD stalls.
Scalability potential: Low devices avoid fixed-cadence I/O; high-end authoring keeps cold recovery.
Hardware Impact: Removes fixed-loop cold I/O risk. No measured microsecond claim.

### D24: Signal lane uniqueness

Problem: Local `AcousticEchoTap` collided with first-party audio contracts, and local haptic/acoustic lanes had no visible bootstrap or consumers.
Solution: Removed local acoustic/haptic signal structs and publishes. SHINOBU now emits canonical `MovementAcousticSignal` and `HapticRequest`. Exertion was renamed to `ShinobuPlayerExertionSignal` and preconfigured in cold startup.
Rejected Alternatives: Keeping duplicate/orphan lanes risks first-push allocation and split nervous-system contracts.
Scalability potential: Low tier emits only threshold events; Ultra consumers can add effects through canonical lanes.
Hardware Impact: Removes first-use queue allocation risk for SHINOBU exertion.

### D25: Fixed-frame hand cadence

Problem: The 3-frame hand ring used `Time.frameCount`; multiple fixed ticks in one rendered frame could alias one slot and corrupt stroke deltas.
Solution: Added `_fixedFrameSequence` and indexed hand samples by fixed simulation sequence.
Rejected Alternatives: Render-frame indexing is not deterministic under variable fixed/update ratios.
Scalability potential: All tiers get stable stroke cadence; high-end can add visual hand overkill without changing truth.
Hardware Impact: No saving claimed; fixes stroke correctness.

### D26: NaN hand ingress

Problem: Provider hand/head vectors could reach NativeArray state unsanitized and poison exertion/fatigue/scratch output.
Solution: Sanitized frame input, hand sample writes, stroke delta lengths, and exertion accumulation before any NativeArray write.
Rejected Alternatives: Trusting OpenXR/provider poses is invalid; one NaN poisons Burst math.
Scalability potential: Low devices degrade to fallback hand positions; high-end tracking remains precise.
Hardware Impact: Small scalar guard cost; prevents catastrophic NaN propagation.

### D27: AUP sync fence

Problem: Origin shifts did not update `ShiftFrameId` or emit an explicit sync fence.
Solution: `OnOriginShift` writes `ShiftFrameId`, mirrors it into blackbox `Reserved`, and publishes `SyncFenceSignal`.
Rejected Alternatives: Relying on sector origin alone hides the shift sequence from postmortem analysis.
Scalability potential: Same deterministic origin tracking on all tiers.
Hardware Impact: One signal on origin-shift events only.

### D28: Provider DataVault sovereignty

Problem: `VRSomaticProvider` owned persistent `NativeArray` buffers for head casts, root sync, hand targets, and blackbox.
Solution: Converted those fields to `VaultNativeArray<T>` views backed by new `BufferID.ShinobuVRSomatic*` DataVault slots.
Rejected Alternatives: Local `new NativeArray` ownership violates H-Phi even if allocation is cold.
Scalability potential: Low/Middle/High/Ultra share DataVault ownership and generation-ready handles.
Hardware Impact: Removes provider-owned native allocations; exact runtime gain is PENDING PROFILER.

